{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_J2n7Iyr8W6NKaV1cWt9Fa3xS","content":"Title: GitHub - Sladck/trainingpeaks-to-intervals-icu: Sync planned TrainingPeaks workouts to intervals.icu with structured power/pace/HR targets\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-05-20\nURL: https://github.com/Sladck/trainingpeaks-to-intervals-icu/\nSnippet: Troubleshooting Permalink: Troubleshooting `AUTH ERROR: TrainingPeaks cookie expired or invalid` Your TP session ended. Log out + back in at trainingpeaks.com, grab a fresh `Production_tpAuth` cookie, update `.env`. `AUTH ERROR: intervals.icu auth failed (HTTP 401)` API key wrong or rotated in icu Developer Settings. A pace target shows as `% pace` instead of `mm:ss/km` Your TrainingPeaks athlete settings have no run / swim threshold pace defined. Set it in TP under Athlete Settings → Pace Zones\n\n---\n\nTitle: running-coach-mcp by carlmichaelkmedieinst\nSite: glama.ai\nDate: 2026-09-05\nURL: https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/carlmichaelkmedieinst/running-coach-mcp\nSnippet: Tools Write tools Writing workouts: why text, not `workout_doc` This project never constructs or sends `workout_doc` directly, for any of the three write tools. Instead, `description` is populated with Intervals.icu's own native workout-builder text syntax ( `src/lib/running/workoutText.ts`'s `generateWorkoutText`), and Intervals.icu's server parses that text into `workout_doc` itself. This is deliberate, not incidental: - It's the officially supported way to create/update structured workouts vi\n\n---\n\nTitle: intervals.icu' – MCP tools\nSite: glama.ai\nDate: 2026-05-13\nURL: https://glama.ai/mcp/tools?query=intervals.icu%27&sourceType=connector\nSnippet: Create or update planned WORKOUT events in Intervals. For changes to an existing STAS workout, keep the same external_id and call this tool again; do not delete the day/window just to edit time, name, note, load, color, or workout_builder. This tool is self-contained; do not look for SKILL.md or examples in the athlete's calendar. dry_run is required: false writes the event, true previews only. Send activity_type as Run/Ride/Swim/Workout/WeightTraining/Yoga/Pilates/Stretching/Walk/Hike/Elliptica\n\n---\n\nTitle: Futureweb Intervals MCP Server | Awesome MCP Servers\nSite: mcpservers.org\nDate: 2026-10-12\nURL: https://mcpservers.org/servers/futureweb/intervals-mcp-server\nSnippet: Tools |Tool|What it does| |-|-| |`analyze_workout_execution`|Planned vs executed per step (duration, target adherence, time in range, HR response, fade, Pw:HR drift with the Intervals.icu decoupling sign, stamina). A step may span any number of laps; with device auto-laps step boundaries inside laps are placed at the intensity change (lap presses are kept), steps without a lap of their own are found between their neighbours, and a caveat with `alignment_confidence` says how far per-step results \n\n---\n\nTitle: intervals.icu MCP Server | MCP Servers\nSite: lobehub.com\nDate: 2025-12-10\nURL: https://lobehub.com/mcp/dballinger-intervals-icu-mcp\nSnippet: MCP Tools Permalink for this section get_planned_workouts Permalink for this section Lists scheduled workouts within a date range. Parameters: - `start_date` (string, required): ISO-8601 format (e.g., \"2025-12-06\") - `end_date` (string, required): ISO-8601 format (e.g., \"2025-12-13\") Returns: Array of planned workouts with: - Workout name and description - Start date - Parsed workout structure with steps and targets (absolute values in watts, bpm, etc.) Example: TypeScript ``` { id: 12345, name:\n\n---\n\nTitle: FIT workout import silently drops REPS steps and mis-scales DISTANCE steps - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2026-08-21\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/fit-workout-import-silently-drops-reps-steps-and-mis-scales-distance-steps/131081\nSnippet: Heading link Related findings (export side, found while testing a `workout_doc` JSON workaround) To work around the above, I tried POSTing `workout_doc` directly in the bulk-events body instead of `file_contents_base64` (bypassing the FIT importer ). That avoids bugs 1 and 2 above — I confirmed it stores step count/duration correctly and does _not_ inject values it wasn’t given — but surfaced th ree more issues on your FIT _export_ side ( `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.fit`), all\n\n---\n\nTitle: Events created via API with `workout_doc`: the web download produces an empty FIT while the API download does not — which representation feeds device sync? - Questions & Help - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2026-09-08\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/events-created-via-api-with-workout-doc-the-web-download-produces-an-empty-fit-while-the-api-download-does-not-which-representation-feeds-device-sync/132326\nSnippet: `5:30/km` needs no athlete-specific reference to be expressed, yet without a threshold it’s dropped entirely rather than exported as-is. And one that would help regardless of the answers: when a planned workout ends up with zero steps, or with every target `OPEN`, is there anything we can check from the API to detect it? Right now `GET` returns a perfectly valid `workout_doc`, so our post-write verification passes while the athlete’s watch is empty. That gap is what made this take a while to fin\n\n---\n\nTitle: Structured running workouts from intervals to Garmin Connect - Uncategorized - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2026-07-17\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/structured-running-workouts-from-intervals-to-garmin-connect/130705\nSnippet: Jul 17, 2026 — ... steps arrive on watch as \"No Target \" (parsed correctly in workout_doc ) ... 1) Basic line format Most steps follow this pattern: - [duration ... Read more\n\n---\n\nTitle: Build and schedule workouts\nSite: icuvisor\nDate: 2026-08-25\nURL: https://icuvisor.app/cookbook/build-workouts/\nSnippet: Add a Press Lap step Permalink for this section To revise an existing template, name it and the assistant uses `update_workout`. On updates, supplied `description` text is prose in the same upstream description/DSL field; it replaces that field rather than appending a note. `workout_doc` is the structured step plan that icuvisor serializes into intervals.icu workout syntax. If the existing template has structured steps you want to keep, ask the assistant to include the desired `workout_doc` expl\n\n---\n\nTitle: FIT File Types Description\nSite: locusmap.eu\nURL: https://help.locusmap.eu/attachments/7598?token=5b577f7a3690a54b281946752bf57914.pdf\nSnippet: The final workout_step\"C\" is at message_index 3, the duration_type is set to 3, indicating the duration_type is\"hr_less_than\" and the duration_value will refer to duration_hr data. This means that the step will be performed for as long as it takes the user's heart rate to drop below that of the specified hr value(in duration_hr).The target_type is set to 4(i.e.power) and the target_value field will refer to target_power_zone data, which is an integer value representing the pre-defined zone. As t\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
