{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_eOkFlC3mJAOWlmja2i92tUP6","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## post by david on May 14, 2024\n\nI am assuming you are using this endpoint to get planned workouts: GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events{format}\n\nYou can specify resolve=true to get actual watts, bpm and m/s values for power, hr and pace steps. These take into account the sport and the athlete’s settings for that sport at that time. This option was only added earlier this year.\n\n## post by Zalachenko on May 14, 2024\n\nI am actualy using default one…without {format}\n\n```lang-auto\nGET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events\n\n```\n\nBut the thing is that I would need everything in % of the reference value(reference value beeing the value that all the workout intervals are referenced by; FTP, LTHR, MaxHR, Threshold Pace, …). So if the reference value is ftp, then I would like to get everything in %FTP, not in exact W, the same for pace, % of Threshold speed. So I can calculate it manualy by using athlete profile and their settings for this values, but that way I need to do 2 API calls.\n\nIt would be nice to always get reference value for that sport type with the actual activity so it is always up to date.\n\n## post by david on May 14, 2024\n\nIf you specify resolve=true then the FTP, LTHR or threshold pace used to convert the workout steps into watts, bpm or m/s are included in the workout\\_doc. These are the athlete’s current settings for the sport.\n\n```lang-auto\n\"workout_doc\": {\n    \"description\": \"A ride done with power\",\n    \"duration\": 3120,\n    \"distance\": 0.0,\n    \"ftp\": 290,\n    \"lthr\": 171,\n    \"target\": \"POWER\",\n\n```\n\nA run done with pace:\n\n```lang-auto\n\"workout_doc\": {\n    \"duration\": 3600,\n    \"distance\": 8310.0,\n    \"lthr\": 171,\n    \"threshold_pace\": 2.7777777,\n    \"pace_units\": \"MINS_KM\",\n    \"target\": \"PACE\",\n    \"steps\": [\n\n```\n\n## post by Zalachenko on May 14, 2024\n\nI see,\n\nThank you for the explanation. It all make sense now!\n\n11 days later\n\n\n## post by Stuart\\_Leitch on May 26, 2024\n\n\nHi, first a HUGE thanks for providing such a good API. It’s so nice to be able to easily dive into my own data\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/39a7d733bfd9e00ee3fd31c2b41c7238.png)\n\nOne of the things that pushed me to this was accidentally plugging my secondary device (bike computer) into my mac while Garmin express was running. So I got a huge number of duplicated activities. Garmin support not really helpful here\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/27075fa54292d163ed7c019e3c95edf3.png)\n\n, and restrict API access to ‘enterprise’ developers. Strava don’t allow activity delete via API\n\n![image3](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/e12ac0c5b6daf872e63ab2633fceacd3.png)\n\nbut did a selective bulk delete for me. But your API allowed me to easily identify and delete the activities pretty easily. Thanks!\n\nOne observation, and a question…\n\n\n`/api/v1/athlete/{id}/activities` is fantastic. But I kinda flinch when giving it a huge date range, especially as there’s a fair amount of data returned per activity. I wondered if you might want to return a list of activity summaries from this endpoint, and the user can then request details on those specific activities they want it for. Also, maybe allowing the list to be filtered server side could help here (e.g. If I only want runs >10km). All of that is making me think about GraphQL, which would be very cool, but obviously non-trivial\n\n![image4](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/39a7d733bfd9e00ee3fd31c2b41c7238.png)\n\nHere’s my question! I’m looking to be able to grab the best efforts of given distances (1km, 1mi, 5km, 10km, HM, Marathon) from a given activity. I don’t quite understand the ‘best-efforts’ endpoint, could you point me to an explanation?\n\nThanks again for a great product. This has really increased my interest and appreciation for Intervals.icu.\n\nStuart\n\n## post by MedTechCD on May 26, 2024\n\nThe ‘Best Efforts’ implementation on the app goes like this:\n\n- Set up the date ranges for which you want Best Efforts results. Power curves on the Activity Power page, or similar Pace Curves on the Activity Pace page\n\n\n\n![image5](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/77c325d2c8ce8f0fb5299ff2e5132070.png)\n\n- Then define the Time ranges for Power or Distance ranges for Pace you’re interested in from the Best Efforts link\n\n\n\n![image6](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/80f98b4e0d9502055f1fb1f291598d7d.png)\n\nThe table now displays all those results for the different time ranges + specifically for the opened activity ‘This Ride/run…’\n\nYou can do the exact same thing for the overall Power and Pace page.\n\nI’m pretty sure that the end-point from the API returns either the activity table or the overall table or probably even both.\n\n## post by david on May 27, 2024\n\nThanks!\n\nYes filtering for the list activities endpoint is probably something I should do. A lot of the other endpoints already support filtering.\n\nYou need to use GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/activity-pace-curves{ext} for your best efforts query.\n\nYou can seen it in action using the inspector by adding a “Best pace” custom chart:\n\n\n![image7](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/5e2cba50c3a57deb4d8db2208e136fd4.png)\n\nGET /api/athlete/2060784/activity-pace-curves?oldest=2023-06-15T00:00:00&newest=2024-06-03T23:59:59&distances=5000&gap=false\n\nJust change /api to /api/v1 to call it yourself. You can specify multiple distances with commas. That endpoint also supports filtering. Add some filters to the tab to see how to do it.\n\nFor one single activity you can fetch its pace curve with GET /api/v1/activity/{id}/pace-curve{ext}\n\n11 days later\n\n\n## post by app4g on Jun 7, 2024\n\n@david let me know if i’m doing something wrong. I’m trying to improve the workout library sync.\n\nFor workouts which I created by myself (either created by hand or dragging existing workouts to the library folder), I have no issues using the API to download the JSON it.\n\nbasically sending a GET to\n\nlet url = “ https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/\\\\(athleteId)/workouts/\\\\(workout.id)”\n\nthen POST to\n\n“ https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/\\\\(athleteId)/download-workoutjson”\n\nHowever, when i copy any workouts that is available from the public workout library into my own Library,\n\neg: Gerries Workout\n\n\n![image8](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/fba24d42edc5e4537197c251fab2c0af.png)\n\nDoing the same process, it will fail with the error:\n\n```lang-auto\nDownload Error: requestError[Error Domain=OAuthSwiftError Code=400 \"Required request body is missing\" UserInfo={NSLocalizedDescription=Required request body is missing, Response-Headers={\n    \"Access-Control-Allow-Origin\" = \"*\";\n    \"Content-Length\" = 57;\n    \"Content-Type\" = \"application/json;charset=UTF-8\";\n    Date = \"Fri, 07 Jun 2024 13:03:29 GMT\";\n    \"Strict-Transport-Security\" = \"max-age=15724800; includeSubDomains\";\n}, OAuthSwiftError.response=<NSHTTPURLResponse: 0x6000037f99c0> { URL: https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athleteId}/download-workoutjson } { Status Code: 400, Headers {\n    \"Access-Control-Allow-Origin\" =     (\n        \"*\"\n    );\n    \"Content-Length\" =     (\n        57\n    );\n    \"Content-Type\" =     (\n        \"application/json;charset=UTF-8\"\n    );\n    Date =     (\n        \"Fri, 07 Jun 2024 13:03:29 GMT\"\n    );\n    \"Strict-Transport-Security\" =     (\n        \"max-age=15724800; includeSubDomains\"\n    );\n} }, OAuthSwiftError.response.data={length = 57, bytes = 0x7b227374 61747573 223a3430 302c2265 ... 69737369 6e67227d }, NSErrorFailingURLKey=https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athleteId}/download-workoutjson, Response-Body={\"status\":400,\"error\":\"Required request body is missing\"}}]\n\n```\n\nwhen issuing a GET to one of my own libraries, i will get a response which can then be converted to JSON. But for the shared libraries, copied to my own library, nothing gets returned.\n\n## post by david on Jun 9, 2024\n\nHmm. I wasn’t able to reproduce this. The `Code=400 \"Required request body is missing\"` response says that you didn’t supply the workout doc etc. in the body of the post so maybe the GET failed?\n\nNote that unfortunately library workout IDs are still “per athlete” (something I should have fixed long ago). So if you are fetching a workout from your library that has been shared by someone else you need to use their athlete id.\n\nSo I have this one in my library (my athlete ID is 2049151):\n\n```\n        {\n            \"athlete_id\": \"5039599\",\n            \"id\": 55,\n            \"icu_training_load\": 28,\n            \"name\": \"Recovery Ride\",\n\n```\n\nTo fetch it I need to do:\n\n```\nGET /api/v1/athlete/5039599/workouts/55\n\n```\n\nTo convert to JSON format for myself:\n\n```\nPOST /api/v1/athlete/2049151/download-workout.json\n(body returned by the GET above)\n\n```\n\n## post by app4g on Jun 9, 2024\n\n15 days later\n\n\n## post by Rik\\_Bruggink on Jun 25, 2024\n\n## post by david on Jun 25, 2024\n\n## post by Rik\\_Bruggink on Jun 25, 2024\n\n14 days later\n\n\n## post by Grego\\_RC on Jul 10, 2024\n\n## post by Grego\\_RC on Jul 11, 2024\n\n## post by david on Jul 11, 2024\n\n14 days later\n\n\n## post by Michael\\_Levy on Jul 26, 2024\n\n## post by app4g on Jul 26, 2024\n\n## post by david on Jul 26, 2024\n\n## post by Bigjuergo on Aug 2, 2024"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_VEkHMaERCosaIxtWmZhrWTOT","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## post by mreno on 12 Aug\n\nHi everyone,\n\nI’m integrating with the intervals.icu API and I’m wondering how to send structured workouts that include repetitions and pace/power ranges.\n\nFor example, I’d like to create a workout like:\n\n- 10 min warm-up\n\n- Repeat 2 times:\n  - 1 km @ 3:30–3:50 min/km\n\n  - 2 min recovery @ 5:10–6:00 min/km\n- 10 min cool-down\n\n\nWhat would be the correct workout structure/format to send this through the API?\n\nIn particular, how should I represent the repeated block and the pace/power ranges for each step?\n\nIs there an example of an API payload or workout format that supports this?\n\nThanks!\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/ce0154fc5b1396e05f5568bf6e75f567.png)\n\n## post by mreno on 12 Aug\n\nI tried this:\n\ncurl -X POST \\\n\n“ https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/i611360/events/bulk?upsert=true” \\\n\n-u “API\\_KEY:$TOKEN” \\\n\n-H “Content-Type: application/json” \\\n\n-d '\\[\n\n{\n\n“category”: “WORKOUT”,\n\n“start\\_date\\_local”: “2026-08-17T00:00:00”,\n\n“type”: “Run”,\n\n“name”: “TEST 2x1km Pace Range V2”,\n\n“description”: “- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace\\\\n\\\\n2x\\\\n- 1km 3:30-3:50/km Pace\\\\n- 2m 5:10-6:00/km Pace\\\\n\\\\n- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace”,\n\n“external\\_id”: “limiar-2x1km-pace-002”\n\n}\n\n\\]’\n\n\\[{“id”:128751902,“start\\_date\\_local”:“2026-08-17T00:00:00”,“icu\\_training\\_load”:null,“icu\\_atl”:null,“icu\\_ctl”:null,“type”:“Run”,“carbs\\_used”:null,“ss\\_p\\_max”:null,“ss\\_w\\_prime”:null,“ss\\_cp”:null,“calendar\\_id”:1,“uid”:“01263f45-93f2-42bf-bee2-872fdcede371”,“athlete\\_id”:“i611360”,“category”:“WORKOUT”,“end\\_date\\_local”:“2026-08-18T00:00:00”,“name”:“TEST 2x1km Pace Range V2”,“description”:“- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace\\\\n\\\\n2x\\\\n- 1km 3:30-3:50/km Pace\\\\n- 2m 5:10-6:00/km Pace\\\\n\\\\n- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace”,“indoor”:null,“color”:null,“moving\\_time”:1880,“icu\\_ftp”:null,“w\\_prime”:null,“p\\_max”:null,“atl\\_days”:null,“ctl\\_days”:null,“updated”:“2026-08-11T22:28:09.608+00:00”,“not\\_on\\_fitness\\_chart”:false,“show\\_as\\_note”:false,“show\\_on\\_ctl\\_line”:false,“for\\_week”:false,“target”:null,“joules”:null,“joules\\_above\\_ftp”:null,“workout\\_doc”:{“steps”:\\[{“pace”:{“end”:376,“start”:330,“units”:“secs/km”},“duration”:600},{“reps”:2,“text”:“2x”,“steps”:\\[{“pace”:{“end”:230,“start”:210,“units”:“secs/km”},“distance”:1000,“duration”:220},{“pace”:{“end”:360,“start”:310,“units”:“secs/km”},“duration”:120}\\],“distance”:2720.430107526882,“duration”:680},{“pace”:{“end”:376,“start”:330,“units”:“secs/km”},“duration”:600}\\],“locales”:\\[\\],“options”:{},“distance”:6134.356606559764,“duration”:1880,“strain\\_score”:null,“average\\_watts”:0,“normalized\\_power”:0,“variability\\_index”:null,“polarization\\_index”:null},“push\\_errors”:null,“athlete\\_cannot\\_edit”:false,“hide\\_from\\_athlete”:false,“structure\\_read\\_only”:false,“created\\_by\\_id”:“i611360”,“shared\\_event\\_id”:null,“entered”:false,“carbs\\_per\\_hour”:null,“sub\\_type”:null,“distance”:6134.3564,“tags”:null,“attachments”:null,“oauth\\_client\\_id”:null,“external\\_id”:“limiar-2x1km-pace-002”,“load\\_target”:null,“time\\_target”:null,“distance\\_target”:null,“training\\_availability”:“NORMAL”,“max\\_training\\_time”:null,“can\\_train\\_sports”:null,“plan\\_athlete\\_id”:null,“plan\\_folder\\_id”:null,“plan\\_workout\\_id”:null,“plan\\_applied”:null,“icu\\_intensity”:null,“strain\\_score”:null,“plan\\_name”:null}\\] **%**\n\ncurl \\\n\n“ https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/i611360/events/128751902” \\\n\n-u “API\\_KEY:$TOKEN”\n\n{“id”:128751902,“start\\_date\\_local”:“2026-08-17T00:00:00”,“icu\\_training\\_load”:null,“icu\\_atl”:null,“icu\\_ctl”:null,“type”:“Run”,“carbs\\_used”:null,“ss\\_p\\_max”:null,“ss\\_w\\_prime”:null,“ss\\_cp”:null,“calendar\\_id”:1,“uid”:“01263f45-93f2-42bf-bee2-872fdcede371”,“athlete\\_id”:“i611360”,“category”:“WORKOUT”,“end\\_date\\_local”:“2026-08-18T00:00:00”,“name”:“TEST 2x1km Pace Range V2”,“description”:“- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace\\\\n\\\\n2x\\\\n- 1km 3:30-3:50/km Pace\\\\n- 2m 5:10-6:00/km Pace\\\\n\\\\n- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace”,“indoor”:null,“color”:null,“moving\\_time”:1880,“icu\\_ftp”:null,“w\\_prime”:null,“p\\_max”:null,“atl\\_days”:null,“ctl\\_days”:null,“updated”:“2026-08-11T22:28:09.608+00:00”,“not\\_on\\_fitness\\_chart”:false,“show\\_as\\_note”:false,“show\\_on\\_ctl\\_line”:false,“for\\_week”:false,“target”:null,“joules”:null,“joules\\_above\\_ftp”:null,“workout\\_doc”:{“steps”:\\[{“pace”:{“end”:376,“start”:330,“units”:“secs/km”},“duration”:600},{“reps”:2,“text”:“2x”,“steps”:\\[{“pace”:{“end”:230,“start”:210,“units”:“secs/km”},“distance”:1000,“duration”:220},{“pace”:{“end”:360,“start”:310,“units”:“secs/km”},“duration”:120}\\],“distance”:2720.430107526882,“duration”:680},{“pace”:{“end”:376,“start”:330,“units”:“secs/km”},“duration”:600}\\],“locales”:\\[\\],“options”:{},“distance”:6134.356606559764,“duration”:1880,“strain\\_score”:null,“average\\_watts”:0,“normalized\\_power”:0,“variability\\_index”:null,“polarization\\_index”:null},“push\\_errors”:null,“athlete\\_cannot\\_edit”:false,“hide\\_from\\_athlete”:false,“structure\\_read\\_only”:false,“created\\_by\\_id”:“i611360”,“shared\\_event\\_id”:null,“entered”:false,“carbs\\_per\\_hour”:null,“sub\\_type”:null,“distance”:6134.3564,“tags”:null,“attachments”:null,“oauth\\_client\\_id”:null,“external\\_id”:“limiar-2x1km-pace-002”,“load\\_target”:null,“time\\_target”:null,“distance\\_target”:null,“training\\_availability”:“NORMAL”,“max\\_training\\_time”:null,“can\\_train\\_sports”:null,“plan\\_athlete\\_id”:null,“plan\\_folder\\_id”:null,“plan\\_workout\\_id”:null,“plan\\_applied”:null,“icu\\_intensity”:null,“strain\\_score”:null,“plan\\_name”:null} **%**\n\nThe `workout_doc` looks correct. However, when I open the workout on my Garmin, I only see the duration. The pace range is missing. Is the `pace` field in `workout_doc` supposed to be converted to a pace target when the workout is pushed to Garmin? The same problem when upload a FIT file.\n\n## post by R2Tom on 12 Aug\n\nCheck this guide\n\nIf it’s not working, show a screenshot of your opened workout on the calendar and of the settings page (pace zones for running)\n\n## post by mreno on 12 Aug\n\nIf my pace zones aren’t configured, the workout ignores the pace targets and uses only the laps. Is this intentional behavior, or is it a bug?\n\n## post by MedTechCD on 12 Aug\n\nThat’s intentional. Both Pace and Power targets are sent as a % of threshold. This makes workouts portable over longer time (where threshold changes) and in between athletes with different thresholds.\n\nSo you need threshold and zones to be configured.\n\n## post by mreno on 12 Aug\n\nActually, I observed something different. If I send a specific pace range, such as 3:55/km–4:10/km, and don’t have pace zones configured in Intervals.icu, the pace range is ignored. But if I configure any pace value (for example, 6:00/km), the specific 3:55–4:10/km range is sent correctly. So it seems that the configured zone is required, but the workout target itself remains an absolute pace range rather than being converted to a percentage.\n\n## post by MedTechCD on 12 Aug\n\nYou can use absolute pace and power values but for the reasons above, it is discouraged.\n\nYou need to set threshold and zones in Intervals to get correct load and full analysis. No way around that.\n\nI don’t know exactly how absolute Pace is handled but for absolute Power (on Garmin - not every brand), you have to send a value over 1000. Any value below 1000 is interpreted as a percentage of threshold. For values above 1000, the first digit (the 1) is dropped and the rest is used as absolute power target value.\n\n- 50 = 50%\n- 100 = 100%\n- 400 = 400%\n- 1050 = 50W\n- 1100 = 100W\n- 11100 = 1100W\n\nBut without threshold and zones, Intervals can’t attribute a Load to a workout with absolute values, so it won’t work."}
